{{define "private_content"}}
<header class="private-page-heading">
  <div><p class="private-kicker">Book administration</p><h1>How to run the book</h1></div>
  <p>A quick guide to the day-to-day flow. Keep this page open the first few times.</p>
</header>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-roles">
  <h2 id="help-roles">Who can do what</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Member</strong> — browses open markets and places wagers.</li>
    <li><strong>Admin</strong> — everything a member can, plus creating markets, accepting/rejecting wagers, settling markets, managing competition setup, inviting members, and linking member accounts to historical players.</li>
    <li><strong>Owner</strong> — everything an admin can, plus inviting admins and changing or revoking roles on the <a href="/admin/members">Members</a> page.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If your session expires while you submit a form, sign in again. You will return to the <strong>Member book</strong> rather than an action URL; for safety, the expired submission is not replayed. Return to the page, confirm that nothing changed, and submit the form again.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-matches">
  <h2 id="help-matches">Setting up teams and matches</h2>
  <p>On <a href="/admin/matches">Matches</a>, create an event and add its teams. In the <strong>Players</strong> section, enter each new golfer's name and click <strong>Add player</strong>; then add each golfer to that event's team roster and designate at least one captain per staffed team. The first golfer added is automatically a captain. A golfer can belong to only one team in an event. Roster changes are audited, and a golfer with match history for that team cannot be removed.</p>
  <p>Then create each match from its staffed team rosters. Both teams must have a captain. Tick each golfer in the checkbox list: <strong>Singles need exactly one rostered player per side</strong>; <strong>Fourball, Foursomes, and Scramble need exactly two per side</strong>; Other needs at least one. Selecting a team filters the golfer list to that roster, and the form checks the first available golfer by default. The server rejects a missing captain or a missing, inactive, duplicated, unrostered, or incorrectly counted player.</p>
  <p>If you create the wrong setup record, open its red <strong>Delete</strong> control, enter an audit reason, and confirm. Only unused matches, unused teams, and empty non-historical events can be deleted. Anything with a market, result, media, statistics, roster, or imported history is protected; use the appropriate void or correction workflow instead. Historical players are deliberately not deletable from this page.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-create">
  <h2 id="help-create">Creating a market</h2>
  <p>Go to <a href="/admin/markets/new">Manage markets → Create a market</a>. Choose a type:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Future</strong> or <strong>Prop</strong> — a standalone market (e.g. “Who wins the Cabot Cup?”). Enter a readable name and price for each outcome; the app creates the internal keys.</li>
    <li><strong>Match</strong> — tied to a specific head-to-head match so it can settle <em>automatically</em> from the verified result.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="help-callout"><strong>Match markets need no copied IDs.</strong> First create the match on the <a href="/admin/matches">Matches</a> page with its required players. On the market form choose <strong>Match</strong>, then pick it from the readable list of valid matches without a market. Teams remain visible for context, while the market title and bet outcomes are derived from the assigned player names. Set only the two prices and times. Recording the result later settles the linked market and updates player standings from the same event.</p>
  <p>A future or prop can have as many outcomes as it needs — a leading-points-getter market names every golfer. The form starts with a few rows and <strong>Add another outcome</strong> gives you more, up to 32. Leave unused rows blank; they are ignored.</p>
  <p>Choose the odds sign separately from its numeric magnitude, which works on phone number keyboards. All dates and times shown or entered in the app are <strong>Atlantic time</strong> (ADT in summer, AST in winter). The market is created in <strong>draft</strong>; click <strong>Open</strong> when you're ready to take bets, and <strong>Close</strong> to stop taking them.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-limits">
  <h2 id="help-limits">Limits: keeping a fun line fun</h2>
  <p>Three limits sit on every wager.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Per market, per member.</strong> On the create form, <em>Most one member may have on it</em> caps a novelty line — say $50. It counts everything that member already has riding on that market, so spreading it over several bets does not get around it, and it counts both sides. Each member gets their own allowance. Leave it blank for no limit.</li>
    <li><strong>Per side, per member.</strong> The same cap, but on one outcome only. Use it when the two sides deserve different treatment: $50 on the +750 longshot while the −1200 favourite stays open. Set it per selection on the create form, or later from <a href="/admin/markets">Markets</a> — the market has to be draft or open. Blank or <code>0</code> clears it. A side limit and a market limit both apply when both are set, so the tighter one bites first.</li>
    <li><strong>Maximum payout, book-wide.</strong> No single wager may win more than <strong>$5,000</strong>. This is about what the book would owe rather than what the member puts up: at +2500, a $200 bet wins $5,000, and $250 would be over. It applies to every wager, including one an admin places for a member.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A member sees both stake limits on the board before they bet, and all three explain themselves if a wager is refused. The payout ceiling is an operator setting; ask if it needs to move.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-close-time">
  <h2 id="help-close-time">Moving a market's closing time</h2>
  <p>Tee times move. On <a href="/admin/markets">Manage markets</a>, a draft or open market has a closing time you can change: pick the new time, say why, and hit <strong>Move</strong>. Wagers already on it keep their odds and stand — only how long new money can come in changes.</p>
  <p>The new time has to be in the future. To stop taking action right now, use <strong>Close</strong> instead. A market that has already closed cannot have its time moved, because reopening it would let money in against a result people may already know. Every change is recorded with the old time, the new time, your name and your reason.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-pricing">
  <h2 id="help-pricing">Dynamic pricing (the line moves on its own)</h2>
  <p>New markets have <strong>dynamic pricing on by default</strong>, with a ${{.DefaultLineWeightDollars}} line weight — you don't need to enter anything. As accepted action comes in, the line automatically shortens on the heavily-backed side and lengthens on the other to balance the book. A larger liquidity moves the line less per bet; uncheck the box to keep a fixed line.</p>
  <p class="help-callout"><strong>The line can only travel so far.</strong> Prices taken at different times can be combined, so if a line ran far enough a member could back one side early, wait for the move, back the other side, and be paid whatever the result — free money out of the book. The engine therefore lets each side's price lengthen only by its share of the margin the market opened with, keeping a cushion in reserve. The practical effect: <strong>a thin opening line moves very little.</strong> A −110 / −110 market can travel about two points a side and then stops dead however much action lands; −130 / +100 gets about three. Every outcome gives up the same <em>proportion</em> of its own price, so on a sixteen-runner outright the longshots move in step with the favourites rather than swinging wildly. If you want a livelier line, <strong>open it wider</strong> — there is no setting that gives both big moves and a book that cannot be picked off.</p>
  <p>You can also <strong>move a line by hand</strong> at any time while a market is draft or open: type the new price beside the selection on <a href="/admin/markets">Manage markets</a>, say why, and set it. That sets the price the engine works from, so the move sticks instead of being recomputed away by the next bet, and the rest of the board is repriced from it immediately. Every hand-set move is recorded with your name and reason.</p>
  <p>A bettor is <strong>locked in at the odds they were shown</strong> when they placed. The move only affects the next bettor. If the line moves while a bet is still pending, the bet still fills at the price the member was quoted — <a href="/admin/wagers">Review wagers</a> shows you the live line beside it so you can accept or reject it on purpose.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-wagers">
  <h2 id="help-wagers">Wagers and approval</h2>
  <p>Members place wagers from <a href="/book/markets">Markets</a>. What happens next depends on the stake:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Stakes up to <strong>${{.AutoApproveDollars}}</strong> are <strong>auto-approved</strong> — accepted immediately and the stake is moved to escrow.</li>
    <li>Larger stakes stay <strong>pending</strong> and appear under <a href="/admin/wagers">Review wagers</a>, where you <strong>Accept</strong> or <strong>Reject</strong> (with a reason) each one.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Each pending wager shows the <strong>live line</strong> — the price its selection is offered at right now — beside the price the member locked in. When the locked price pays the member more than today's line, the row is flagged <strong>Moved in member's favour</strong> and a warning appears above the queue, with what the same stake would win at the current line. Nothing is decided for you: <strong>Accept</strong> always fills at the price the member was quoted, so reject the ones you don't want. <strong>Off the board</strong> means the selection or market is no longer open, so there is no live line to compare.</p>
  <p>The <a href="/admin/wagers/record">full wager record</a> lists every wager the book has taken — pending, live, and settled — with three prices side by side: the line the market <strong>opened</strong> at, the price the member <strong>took</strong>, and where the line <strong>closed</strong>. A member who repeatedly takes a better number than the close is beating the book on price whether or not the bets land, and the row says how much better in dollars on that stake. While a market is still open there is no closing line yet, so the comparison is withheld rather than guessed at.</p>
  <p>The member sees the same live line on their own <a href="/book/wagers">Wagers</a> page, and can <strong>Cancel</strong> a wager of theirs that is still pending — useful when the line moved against them while they waited. A cancelled wager shows as <em>cancelled</em> and never staked anything. Once you accept a wager the stake is in escrow and the member can no longer pull it; unwinding it then means voiding or settling the market.</p>
  <p>The auto-approve threshold is a book-wide setting; ask your operator to change it if needed.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-void-wager">
  <h2 id="help-void-wager">Voiding one member's bet</h2>
  <p>Sometimes a single bet should not stand — it was struck in error, or on a number you will not honour for that member — but the rest of the market is fine. On the <a href="/admin/wagers/record">wager record</a>, an accepted bet has a <strong>Void</strong> control: give a reason and the member's stake goes straight back to them. Every other wager on that market is untouched, which is the difference from <strong>Settle / void</strong> on the market itself, which refunds everybody.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Only an <strong>accepted</strong> bet can be voided this way. One still <em>pending</em> is <strong>Rejected</strong> instead — no money has moved, so there is nothing to return.</li>
    <li>A <strong>settled</strong> bet has already paid; correcting that means re-settling the market.</li>
    <li>The stake goes back exactly as it came. Nobody wins or loses: the bet simply never happened.</li>
    <li>The reason appears on the member's ledger beside the refund, and the entry records which admin did it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The line moves back too — a voided stake is no longer action, so the board reprices as though the bet had never been taken.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-settle">
  <h2 id="help-settle">Settling a market</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Match</strong> markets settle automatically once the match result is verified.</li>
    <li><strong>Future</strong> and <strong>Prop</strong> markets are settled by hand: open the market's <strong>Settle / void</strong> page, mark each selection win / loss / push (or void the whole market), and give a reason. Winning wagers are paid from escrow; a void refunds every stake.</li>
    <li><strong>You do not have to close a market first.</strong> Grading a market that is still open closes it in the same step, because grading it says the result is in. <strong>Close</strong> is still there for when you want to stop taking action before you know the answer.</li>
    <li><strong>Settled and voided are final.</strong> Once a market is graded, a wager still sitting in the approval queue for it can no longer be accepted — there is no settlement left to grade it against, so the stake would go into escrow and stay there. Approve pending wagers before you grade, and if one turns up afterwards, decide it with the member directly.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>When an admin records a verified match result, enter the score as it should appear publicly and give the audit reason. The winning golfers, score, verification method, and date remain visible on the Matches page. Verified results automatically appear in the public match history, cup standings, and individual overall/singles/team-format statistics; pending or disputed results do not.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-dashboard">
  <h2 id="help-dashboard">Reading the admin dashboard</h2>
  <p>The <a href="/admin">Admin</a> page is the book's scoreboard. All figures are CAD.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>House result</strong> — what the book has actually won or lost on settled action. <strong>In escrow</strong> is the stake being held on live bets, and <strong>handle</strong> is every dollar of action taken.</li>
    <li><strong>If everything lands</strong> — the worst and best the book can net across every unsettled market, plus the wagers waiting on your approval and the bets already live.</li>
    <li><strong>Exposure by market</strong> — for each outcome: what is staked on it, what it would pay out, and what the house nets (every stake in that market less that side's payout). The row that costs the book most is marked, so you can see where you are one result away from a bad day.</li>
    <li><strong>Player standings</strong> — each member's realized profit or loss against the book, read straight from the ledger, with their record and total action. Bars run right of the line for players who are ahead of the book and left for those behind. Live bets are not counted until they settle.</li>
    <li><strong>Action on the board</strong> — the accepted bets still waiting on a result.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Every member named on the dashboard is a link into <strong>their book</strong> — standings, owed and owing, and the live action list — so you can go from "who bet that?" to their balance and wagers in one click, without detouring through Members. The action list shows <strong>every</strong> live bet, and the <strong>Filter</strong> box narrows it by member, market or pick as you type; the wager record has the same. Filtering happens in the page, so nothing is fetched and nothing is hidden from a browser without JavaScript.</p>
  <p>The dashboard is split into tabs — <strong>Overview</strong>, <strong>Exposure</strong>, <strong>Players</strong>, <strong>Action</strong> and <strong>Ledger health</strong> — so a busy book stays readable. The tab you are on is kept in the address bar, so a refresh or a shared link lands in the same place.</p>
  <p>The ledger state and outbox queues stay under <strong>Ledger health</strong>: if <strong>Unbalanced</strong> is anything other than zero, or failed outbox events pile up, tell your operator.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-member-book">
  <h2 id="help-member-book">Looking at a member's book, and betting for them</h2>
  <p>From <a href="/admin/members">Members</a>, open a member's <strong>book</strong> to see where they stand: their balance, their credit line and how much of it is left, the auto-approve limit that applies to them, every wager they hold, and their ledger. This is another member's money and history, so it is admin-only.</p>
  <p>The same page lets you <strong>place a wager for them</strong> — useful when someone texts you a bet rather than putting it on themselves. Points worth knowing:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The bet is <strong>theirs</strong>: their stake, their balance, their result. The row records that <strong>you</strong> placed it, and their own wagers page tells them so.</li>
    <li>It <strong>fills immediately</strong> rather than waiting in the approval queue — you are the book, so there is nobody left to approve it.</li>
    <li>If their balance and credit will not cover the stake, the wager is <strong>left pending</strong> and you are told why, rather than half-placed.</li>
    <li><strong>Restrictions still apply.</strong> A market or side they are barred from is not offered here either, and a wager on it is refused even when an admin places it.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-restrictions">
  <h2 id="help-restrictions">Keeping a member off a bet</h2>
  <p>When a bet is about someone, they should not be able to back it — and on a two-way prop they often should not be able to back <em>one</em> side of it. On <a href="/admin/markets">Manage markets</a>, open <strong>Restrict a member</strong> on the market, choose the member, choose either <strong>the whole market</strong> or a single outcome, and give a reason.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A <strong>whole-market</strong> restriction hides the market from that member completely.</li>
    <li>A <strong>single outcome</strong> restriction hides just that outcome. They still see the market and can back the other side — so the player whose eagles line it is can take the over but never the under.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Hiding is only the courtesy: a restricted wager is refused by the server as well, so it does not matter if they have the page open from before or go looking for the link. Restrictions are listed under the market table with who set them and why, and <strong>Lift</strong> puts the bet back in front of that member. Lifting a whole-market restriction leaves any single-outcome ones in place.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-settle-up">
  <h2 id="help-settle-up">Settling up: recording a payment or a payout</h2>
  <p>A member's <strong>balance</strong> is what they owe the book or the book owes them. Betting moves it; so does actually paying each other. The <a href="/admin/settle-up">Settle up</a> page is where you record the second kind — money that changed hands outside the app, by e-transfer, cash, or anything else.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Outstanding</strong> lists everyone who is not square. A member who owes shows a negative balance, and their form is pre-filled with the amount and direction that would clear it — you only have to say how the money moved.</li>
    <li><strong>Paid the book</strong> records money coming in from a member; <strong>Paid out to them</strong> records money going out. Either way the note you type is the audit record, so write what actually happened ("e-transfer 25 July").</li>
    <li>Recording a settlement <strong>changes nobody's profit and loss</strong>. The dashboard's house result and player standings count wagers only, so paying up squares the balance and leaves the season's results exactly as they were.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Got one wrong? <strong>Reverse</strong> it with a reason. Ledger history is never deleted: the original entry stays visible and a linked correcting entry cancels it, so the balance returns to where it was and both rows remain on the record. A settlement can only be reversed once.</p>
  <p>Admins and owners can both record and reverse settlements, and every entry keeps the name of whoever made it.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-members">
  <h2 id="help-members">Adding people and linking their golf history</h2>
  <p>On the <a href="/admin/members">Members</a> page, generate a one-time <strong>invite link</strong> and send it to someone. They open it, sign in with Google, and join automatically — no need to pre-enter their email. Admins can invite members; only an owner can invite admins or change roles.</p>
  <p>After they join, use the <strong>Historical player</strong> column to link their member account to the matching golfer. This is a one-to-one mapping: each member and historical player can have only one link. Unlink the current player before changing a mistaken mapping. Linking does not create or alter match results; it connects the login identity to the player whose statistics come from verified matches.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-archive-2025">
  <h2 id="help-archive-2025">Completing the 2025 public archive</h2>
  <p>The public <a href="/history/2025">2025 Cabot Cup page</a> is intentionally blank and marked <strong>Archive in progress</strong>. Betting records and aggregate player totals are not treated as match scorecards, so the page does not guess a winner, teams, score, or individual results.</p>
  <p>When authoritative material is available, enter the teams, rosters, players, matches, and verified results through <a href="/admin/matches">Matches</a>; those confirmed records will populate the placeholder's match and statistics section automatically. Prepare the tournament write-up and selected photographs separately. The placeholder has reserved sections for all three.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-test-mode">
  <h2 id="help-test-mode">Testing without touching the live ledger</h2>
  <p>The production process always uses the real database. Do not switch that process to test mode: sessions, identities, wagers, settlements, and the outbox worker all use the selected database together.</p>
  <p>Use the separate <a href="https://test.cabotcup.ca">Cabot Cup acceptance site</a> to rehearse match results, automatic settlement, voids, reversals, and migration checks. Its private pages display a full-width red <strong>Test book</strong> banner. It runs with <code>APP_ENV=staging</code> and <code>DATABASE_MODE=test</code> against the isolated <code>cabot_cup_test</code> database. The acceptance site uses normal Google sign-in and has its own memberships, sessions, wagers, ledger, and outbox; nothing entered there appears in the live book.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-overview">
  <h2 id="help-overview">What a member sees on their overview</h2>
  <p>The <a href="/book">Member book</a> overview answers "where do I stand and what have I got riding" without anyone having to decode a ledger reference:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Balances and limits</strong> — available cash (negative means money owed to the book, drawn against their credit line), credit still available to bet against their credit limit, and <strong>their own auto-approve limit</strong>, so they know before placing a wager whether it fills instantly or waits for you. A per-player override set on the <a href="/admin/members">Members</a> page shows there instead of the book default, and an override of <code>0</code> reads as "every wager waits for admin approval".</li>
    <li><strong>Open / Pending approval / Settled</strong> counts, each with the money behind it: stake at risk and what it would return if it all lands, and the stake sitting in the approval queue.</li>
    <li><strong>What you have riding</strong> — their open and pending wagers by market, pick, locked-in odds, stake, and payout, with the pending ones flagged. Only the most recent few are listed; the rest are on their <a href="/book/wagers">Wagers</a> page.</li>
    <li><strong>Recent activity</strong> — each ledger line names the market and pick behind it rather than showing a bare wager ID. The full <a href="/book/ledger">Ledger</a> still shows the underlying reference for audit.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Everything here is scoped to the signed-in member; nobody sees another member's balances or wagers from these pages.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section" aria-labelledby="help-settings">
  <h2 id="help-settings">Current settings &amp; defaults</h2>
  <dl class="help-settings">
    <dt>Auto-approve threshold (book default)</dt>
    <dd>${{.AutoApproveDollars}} — wagers up to this stake are accepted automatically. Set per-player overrides on the <a href="/admin/members">Members</a> page (blank uses this default; <code>0</code> forces every wager to manual review). Change the book default via the <code>WAGER_AUTO_APPROVE_MAX_CENTS</code> deployment setting.</dd>
    <dt>Dynamic pricing line weight (default)</dt>
    <dd>${{.DefaultLineWeightDollars}} — used when you enable dynamic pricing without entering a value. A larger weight moves the line less per bet; set it per market on the create form.</dd>
    <dt>Credit line (per player)</dt>
    <dd>Every member starts with a ${{.DefaultCreditDollars}} credit limit — they can bet until they owe the book that much (their balance goes negative). When someone "applies for extra", raise their credit limit on the <a href="/admin/members">Members</a> page. Change the starting figure via the <code>DEFAULT_CREDIT_LIMIT_CENTS</code> deployment setting; that moves new members only, not people who already have an account.</dd>
    <dt>Currencies</dt>
    <dd>Markets are priced and settled in a single currency (CAD or USD), chosen when the market is created.</dd>
  </dl>
</section>
{{end}}
